-- Author-requested explanation of reading the attached CV.
update public.articles set sections = '{"introduction:season": "It’s that time of year again: graduate admissions season.", "introduction:0": "Every year, I receive many emails from prospective graduate students interested in joining my lab. Some catch my attention. Many do not. This doesn’t mean the students are not a fit, but they have failed to make a compelling case for me to invest more time in learning about them. In the end, it’s a loss for both of us.", "introduction:1": "The difference is not fancy formatting or perfect prose. It is much simpler: Has the student made a convincing case that they are genuinely interested in my lab and the type of supervision I can provide? Is there something in their email that suggests they could succeed in a graduate program? Do they show the potential to conduct independent research?", "introduction:application-costs": "We professors get it. Students are trying to avoid mounting application costs while also looking for an edge in a competitive admissions process.", "introduction:2": "This matters even more in the age of AI. It has never been easier to generate a polished email that sounds professional while saying almost nothing.", "introduction:3": "So, how should you email a professor about a graduate position? Here are my answers to some common questions. I recognize that different people are looking for different things. I’m offering my perspective, which I suspect is shared by many other professors. Have a differing opinion? Share your thoughts as a note. I’m always looking to learn from others.", "where-should-i-start:0": "Don’t start by targeting institutions. Start by identifying professors whose research resonates with your interests.", "where-should-i-start:1": "In computer science, one starting point is CSRankings.org, where you can explore faculty publication activity in particular research areas.", "where-should-i-start:2": "Choose the areas that interest you, look for active researchers, and then explore their personal and lab webpages, and recent papers.", "do-professors-actually-read-these-emails:0": "Yes. At least I do.", "do-professors-actually-read-these-emails:1": "But professors receive a lot of email, and prospective-student emails are competing for attention with everything else in the inbox.", "do-professors-actually-read-these-emails:email-time": "Like everyone else, a professor''s email time is limited. Most do not have an administrative assistant to wade through mountains of emails. Every minute on email takes away from their own students. The question is: how do you differentiate yourself amid the onslaught of generic, AI-generated, templated emails?", "do-professors-actually-read-these-emails:2": "You don’t need a clever subject line or an elaborate introduction. You need to quickly give me a reason to keep reading.", "what-should-i-do-before-emailing:0": "Do your due diligence. Yes, that means homework.", "what-should-i-do-before-emailing:1": "Start with the professor’s webpage. What does their lab work on? Are they accepting students? Have they provided instructions for prospective students? What have they published recently?", "what-should-i-do-before-emailing:2": "A professor’s email is not a general information centre.", "what-should-i-do-before-emailing:3": "If the answer to your question is prominently displayed on my webpage, asking me the same question in an email is not a great first impression.", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:0": "There is nothing wrong with contacting multiple professors. Definitely do not CC the entire department, as some occasionally do. You are looking for a graduate supervisor, and finding a good match matters.", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:advisor-fit": "You are putting an important part of your training and career in an advisor’s hands. The goal is not simply to get any graduate position, but to find one where you can learn from your advisor, grow as a researcher, and thrive.", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:1": "But there is a difference between contacting several professors whose research genuinely interests you and sending the same email to 100 faculty members.", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:2": "Professors can tell.", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:3": "You are not trying to maximize the number of emails you send. You are trying to maximize the chance that the right professor reads your email and thinks:", "should-i-email-lots-of-professors:4": "This person might be a good fit for my lab.", "my-friend-sent-many-emails:0": "That doesn’t mean this is the optimal way to approach the admissions process. It reminds me of stories about famous tech entrepreneurs who dropped out of university. That’s survivorship bias. For most, a well-crafted, personalized email has a better chance of catching a prof’s attention. There’s a lot of luck involved in this process, and it’s best to put yourself in the right place for that luck to strike.", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:0": "Personalized does not mean:", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:1": "Dear Professor [NAME],\nI am deeply impressed by your groundbreaking research in [RESEARCH AREA].", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:2": "Changing two fields in a template is not personalization.", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:3": "Tell me why you are contacting me.", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:4": "Maybe one of my recent papers connects to a project you worked on. Maybe my lab works on a problem you have been thinking about. Maybe you have experience that would be particularly relevant to something we are doing.", "how-personalized-should-the-email-be:5": "You don’t need a paragraph praising my research. One specific, thoughtful sentence can be much more effective.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:0": "If it genuinely interested you, absolutely. If it’s just a random paper you selected from the professor’s publication list, please don’t. It’s painfully obvious when this is done, and it does not reflect well on you.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:older-paper-example": "It’s not uncommon for a student to reference a paper I worked on in 2015. That was over a decade ago! In arXiv years, that’s like a century. My research interests have moved on.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:recent-directions": "Focus on recent work. Better yet, try to anticipate where it might go next. What questions remain open? What would be a natural next step? Show me that you are thinking about where we can take this research together as well as what it has already accomplished.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:1": "But don’t just tell me that you read it.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:2": "Tell me something about it.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:3": "What did you find interesting? How does it connect to your previous work? Did it make you think about a related problem?", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:4": "“I read your paper on X and found it fascinating” tells me very little.", "should-i-mention-one-of-the-professor-s-papers:5": "A specific observation tells me that you actually engaged with the work.", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:0": "Give me the information that helps me answer one question:", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:1": "Why might this person be a good researcher in my lab?", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:2": "That could include research experience, a thesis, an interesting project, publications, relevant industry experience, open-source contributions, or other evidence that you can tackle difficult problems.", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:signal-of-success": "Ultimately, I am looking for signals that you can succeed in graduate research. Those signals might come from advanced courses you have taken, research experience, work experience, or other parts of your background. But the strongest ones are those that connect in some way to the research being done in my lab.", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:3": "You don’t need to tell me everything you have ever done.", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:4": "This is an email, not your autobiography.", "what-should-i-tell-the-professor-about-myself:5": "Highlight the things that matter for this particular lab and let your CV provide the details.", "how-long-should-the-email-be:0": "Short. Very short.", "how-long-should-the-email-be:1": "A professor should be able to understand three things very quickly:", "how-long-should-the-email-be:2": "Who are you?", "how-long-should-the-email-be:3": "Why are you contacting me?", "how-long-should-the-email-be:4": "Why might there be a research fit?", "how-long-should-the-email-be:5": "If answering those questions requires scrolling through several screens of text, your email is probably too long.", "how-long-should-the-email-be:statement-of-purpose": "Don’t simply copy and paste your general statement of purpose into the email.", "how-long-should-the-email-be:6": "Respect the reader’s time.", "should-i-attach-my-cv:0": "Yes, unless the professor says otherwise.", "should-i-attach-my-cv:piqued-interest": "Of course, if your email piques my interest, I immediately go to the attached CV.", "should-i-attach-my-cv:1": "Make it easy for me to learn more about you.", "should-i-attach-my-cv:2": "If you have a personal webpage, Google Scholar profile, GitHub repository, or other relevant work, include links where appropriate.", "should-i-attach-my-cv:3": "Don’t make me hunt for information.", "what-about-my-grades:0": "Strong grades can certainly help, particularly when they are in relevant courses.", "what-about-my-grades:1": "But graduate research is not simply an extension of taking courses. I am also looking for evidence of curiosity, independence, technical ability, persistence, and the ability to think about open-ended problems.", "what-about-my-grades:2": "Your email should help me see the researcher behind the transcript.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:0": "Of course.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:1": "But be careful.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:2": "AI has made it incredibly easy to produce emails that are grammatically perfect, professionally worded, and completely forgettable.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:3": "In fact, polished generic writing is becoming less informative precisely because it is now so cheap to produce.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:4": "Use AI to help you communicate your ideas clearly. Don’t use it to manufacture interest you don’t have or understanding you haven’t developed.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:5": "AI can improve your writing. It cannot do your homework for you.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:6": "The strongest signal in your email is increasingly not polish.", "can-i-use-ai-to-write-the-email:7": "It is specificity.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:personal-stance": "Personally, no. I get all the emails, trust me.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:2": "Professors are busy. They may not have funding. They may already have enough students. They may be travelling. They may simply have missed your email.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:funding": "I fund all my students at a level that allows them to focus on their research. That means my funding can only support a limited number of students, as much as I would like to take on more.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:lab-size": "I also value my time. I keep my lab relatively small, with five or six graduate students at a time, so I can work closely with each of them.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:3": "And sometimes they are not interested as they don''t see a fit with their lab.", "should-i-send-a-follow-up-if-i-don-t-hear-back:4": "No response is frustrating, but repeatedly emailing is unlikely to change the outcome.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:0": "This is important.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:1": "Your goal is probably not to convince a professor to admit you based on one email.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:2": "Your goal is to make them curious enough to take the next step.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:3": "Open your CV.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:4": "Click on your webpage.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:5": "Look at your research.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:6": "Reply to your email.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:7": "Perhaps arrange a conversation.", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:teaser-and-effort": "Think of your email as a teaser: you want to pique the professor’s interest and make them want to learn more. If you don’t put effort into your email, why would you expect the professor to put effort into learning more about you?", "what-am-i-actually-trying-to-accomplish-with-this-email:8": "Think of the email as opening a door, not closing a deal.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:0": "There is no magic template, and that is partly the point.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:1": "A good email is short, specific, and genuine. It shows that you know who you are writing to. It gives the professor enough information to understand why there might be a match. And it makes it easy to learn more about you.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:2": "Don’t obsess over finding the perfect words.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:llm-flow": "Once you have written a genuine letter in your own words, an LLM can help improve its flow. Start with your own ideas and reasons for reaching out, then use it to help you express them more clearly.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:3": "Do your homework. Keep it short. Make it personal. Give the professor a reason to want to know more.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:4": "You may only get one shot at catching their attention.", "so-what-does-a-good-email-look-like:5": "Make it count.", "article-body": "It’s that time of year again: graduate admissions season.\n\nEvery year, I receive many emails from prospective graduate students interested in joining my lab. Some catch my attention. Many do not. This doesn’t mean the students are not a fit, but they have failed to make a compelling case for me to invest more time in learning about them. In the end, it’s a loss for both of us.\n\nThe difference is not fancy formatting or perfect prose. It is much simpler: Has the student made a convincing case that they are genuinely interested in my lab and the type of supervision I can provide? Is there something in their email that suggests they could succeed in a graduate program? Do they show the potential to conduct independent research?\n\nWe professors get it. Students are trying to avoid mounting application costs while also looking for an edge in a competitive admissions process.\n\nThis matters even more in the age of AI. It has never been easier to generate a polished email that sounds professional while saying almost nothing.\n\nSo, how should you email a professor about a graduate position? Here are my answers to some common questions. I recognize that different people are looking for different things. I’m offering my perspective, which I suspect is shared by many other professors. Have a differing opinion? Share your thoughts as a note. I’m always looking to learn from others.\n\nWhere should I start?\n\nDon’t start by targeting institutions. Start by identifying professors whose research resonates with your interests.\n\nIn computer science, one starting point is CSRankings.org, where you can explore faculty publication activity in particular research areas.\n\nChoose the areas that interest you, look for active researchers, and then explore their personal and lab webpages, and recent papers.\n\nDo professors actually read these emails?\n\nYes. At least I do.\n\nBut professors receive a lot of email, and prospective-student emails are competing for attention with everything else in the inbox.\n\nLike everyone else, a professor''s email time is limited. Most do not have an administrative assistant to wade through mountains of emails. Every minute on email takes away from their own students. The question is: how do you differentiate yourself amid the onslaught of generic, AI-generated, templated emails?\n\nYou don’t need a clever subject line or an elaborate introduction. You need to quickly give me a reason to keep reading.\n\nWhat should I do before emailing?\n\nDo your due diligence. Yes, that means homework.\n\nStart with the professor’s webpage. What does their lab work on? Are they accepting students? Have they provided instructions for prospective students? What have they published recently?\n\nA professor’s email is not a general information centre.\n\nIf the answer to your question is prominently displayed on my webpage, asking me the same question in an email is not a great first impression.\n\nShould I email lots of professors?\n\nThere is nothing wrong with contacting multiple professors. Definitely do not CC the entire department, as some occasionally do. You are looking for a graduate supervisor, and finding a good match matters.\n\nYou are putting an important part of your training and career in an advisor’s hands. The goal is not simply to get any graduate position, but to find one where you can learn from your advisor, grow as a researcher, and thrive.\n\nBut there is a difference between contacting several professors whose research genuinely interests you and sending the same email to 100 faculty members.\n\nProfessors can tell.\n\nYou are not trying to maximize the number of emails you send. You are trying to maximize the chance that the right professor reads your email and thinks:\n\nThis person might be a good fit for my lab.\n\nMy friend sent many emails and it worked for them. Why shouldn’t I do it?\n\nThat doesn’t mean this is the optimal way to approach the admissions process. It reminds me of stories about famous tech entrepreneurs who dropped out of university. That’s survivorship bias. For most, a well-crafted, personalized email has a better chance of catching a prof’s attention. There’s a lot of luck involved in this process, and it’s best to put yourself in the right place for that luck to strike.\n\nHow personalized should the email be?\n\nPersonalized does not mean:\n\nDear Professor [NAME],\nI am deeply impressed by your groundbreaking research in [RESEARCH AREA].\n\nChanging two fields in a template is not personalization.\n\nTell me why you are contacting me.\n\nMaybe one of my recent papers connects to a project you worked on. Maybe my lab works on a problem you have been thinking about. Maybe you have experience that would be particularly relevant to something we are doing.\n\nYou don’t need a paragraph praising my research. One specific, thoughtful sentence can be much more effective.\n\nShould I mention one of the professor’s papers?\n\nIf it genuinely interested you, absolutely. If it’s just a random paper you selected from the professor’s publication list, please don’t. It’s painfully obvious when this is done, and it does not reflect well on you.\n\nIt’s not uncommon for a student to reference a paper I worked on in 2015. That was over a decade ago! In arXiv years, that’s like a century. My research interests have moved on.\n\nFocus on recent work. Better yet, try to anticipate where it might go next. What questions remain open? What would be a natural next step? Show me that you are thinking about where we can take this research together as well as what it has already accomplished.\n\nBut don’t just tell me that you read it.\n\nTell me something about it.\n\nWhat did you find interesting? How does it connect to your previous work? Did it make you think about a related problem?\n\n“I read your paper on X and found it fascinating” tells me very little.\n\nA specific observation tells me that you actually engaged with the work.\n\nWhat should I tell the professor about myself?\n\nGive me the information that helps me answer one question:\n\nWhy might this person be a good researcher in my lab?\n\nThat could include research experience, a thesis, an interesting project, publications, relevant industry experience, open-source contributions, or other evidence that you can tackle difficult problems.\n\nUltimately, I am looking for signals that you can succeed in graduate research. Those signals might come from advanced courses you have taken, research experience, work experience, or other parts of your background. But the strongest ones are those that connect in some way to the research being done in my lab.\n\nYou don’t need to tell me everything you have ever done.\n\nThis is an email, not your autobiography.\n\nHighlight the things that matter for this particular lab and let your CV provide the details.\n\nHow long should the email be?\n\nShort. Very short.\n\nA professor should be able to understand three things very quickly:\n\nWho are you?\n\nWhy are you contacting me?\n\nWhy might there be a research fit?\n\nIf answering those questions requires scrolling through several screens of text, your email is probably too long.\n\nDon’t simply copy and paste your general statement of purpose into the email.\n\nRespect the reader’s time.\n\nShould I attach my CV?\n\nYes, unless the professor says otherwise.\n\nOf course, if your email piques my interest, I immediately go to the attached CV.\n\nMake it easy for me to learn more about you.\n\nIf you have a personal webpage, Google Scholar profile, GitHub repository, or other relevant work, include links where appropriate.\n\nDon’t make me hunt for information.\n\nWhat about my grades?\n\nStrong grades can certainly help, particularly when they are in relevant courses.\n\nBut graduate research is not simply an extension of taking courses. I am also looking for evidence of curiosity, independence, technical ability, persistence, and the ability to think about open-ended problems.\n\nYour email should help me see the researcher behind the transcript.\n\nCan I use AI to write the email?\n\nOf course.\n\nBut be careful.\n\nAI has made it incredibly easy to produce emails that are grammatically perfect, professionally worded, and completely forgettable.\n\nIn fact, polished generic writing is becoming less informative precisely because it is now so cheap to produce.\n\nUse AI to help you communicate your ideas clearly. Don’t use it to manufacture interest you don’t have or understanding you haven’t developed.\n\nAI can improve your writing. It cannot do your homework for you.\n\nThe strongest signal in your email is increasingly not polish.\n\nIt is specificity.\n\nShould I send a follow-up if I don’t hear back?\n\nPersonally, no. I get all the emails, trust me.\n\nProfessors are busy. They may not have funding. They may already have enough students. They may be travelling. They may simply have missed your email.\n\nI fund all my students at a level that allows them to focus on their research. That means my funding can only support a limited number of students, as much as I would like to take on more.\n\nI also value my time. I keep my lab relatively small, with five or six graduate students at a time, so I can work closely with each of them.\n\nAnd sometimes they are not interested as they don''t see a fit with their lab.\n\nNo response is frustrating, but repeatedly emailing is unlikely to change the outcome.\n\nWhat am I actually trying to accomplish with this email?\n\nThis is important.\n\nYour goal is probably not to convince a professor to admit you based on one email.\n\nYour goal is to make them curious enough to take the next step.\n\nOpen your CV.\n\nClick on your webpage.\n\nLook at your research.\n\nReply to your email.\n\nPerhaps arrange a conversation.\n\nThink of your email as a teaser: you want to pique the professor’s interest and make them want to learn more. If you don’t put effort into your email, why would you expect the professor to put effort into learning more about you?\n\nThink of the email as opening a door, not closing a deal.\n\nSo what does a good email look like?\n\nThere is no magic template, and that is partly the point.\n\nA good email is short, specific, and genuine. It shows that you know who you are writing to. It gives the professor enough information to understand why there might be a match. And it makes it easy to learn more about you.\n\nDon’t obsess over finding the perfect words.\n\nOnce you have written a genuine letter in your own words, an LLM can help improve its flow. Start with your own ideas and reasons for reaching out, then use it to help you express them more clearly.\n\nDo your homework. Keep it short. Make it personal. Give the professor a reason to want to know more.\n\nYou may only get one shot at catching their attention.\n\nMake it count."}'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where id = 'email-professor';
